# `tensor()` construction over PACKED buffers (Stage 3n). Converting a nested 1024x1024 array
# cost 0.12s — about 120 ns per element for what is fundamentally an 8 MB memcpy, and more than
# the 1024^3 GEMM that followed it. The cause was `ArrayData::to_values()`, which materializes a
# `Vec<Value>`: on a packed float buffer that means allocating a box per element. It ran TWICE
# per row — once in `shape_of` purely to re-derive each element's (empty) shape and discard it,
# once in `flatten_into` to copy — so a 1024x1024 build paid ~2M allocations.
#
# `shape_of` now short-circuits a packed Ints/Floats/Range buffer to `[len]` without walking it,
# and `flatten_into` copies straight through. That is sound because a packed buffer holds
# numbers by construction, so the element walk it skips could never have rejected anything —
# `missing` and non-numeric elements only ever live in an `ArrayData::Values`, which still takes
# the original path.
#
# The SHARP EDGE is ragged detection, because only NON-FIRST rows are compared against the
# first: a mistake shows in one nesting orientation and not the other. Those rejection cases
# live in the Rust test (they raise, which would stop this file); what is pinned here is that
# every accepted shape still produces the right VALUES in the right ORDER — a row/column swap
# would slip past a shape-only check.

# ---- 1-D sources, one per packed representation ----

# a packed Floats buffer: the extend_from_slice path
print(tensor([1.5, 2.5, 3.5]))

# a packed Ints buffer: widened to f64 on the way in
print(tensor([1, 2, 3]))

# negative and large ints, either side of 2^53 — the widening is a real conversion, and
# 2^53+1 has no f64 representation, so it lands on the even neighbour
print(tensor([0 - 5, 0, 9007199254740993]))

# a LAZY range source, never materialized as a Vec<i64> first
print(tensor(0..5))

# a range with a negative step: the element formula must match `range_at` exactly
print(tensor(range(10, 0, 0 - 2)))

# an empty buffer keeps the [0] shape the element walk used to produce
print(tensor([]).shape())

# a bare scalar is rank 0, not rank 1
print(tensor(5).shape())

# ---- 2-D and 3-D: shape AND element order ----

# element order is the assertion here; a row/column mix-up prints differently
print(tensor([[1, 2], [3, 4]]))
print(tensor([[1, 2], [3, 4]]).shape())

# a non-square matrix, so a transposed flatten cannot pass by symmetry
print(tensor([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]))
print(tensor([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]).shape())

# rank 3
print(tensor([[[1, 2]], [[3, 4]]]).shape())

# rows of different element types: one packed Ints, one packed Floats, both widened
print(tensor([[1, 2], [3.5, 4.5]]))

# ---- the k8 path end to end: build by nested map, convert, multiply ----
# Every stage here was a separate defect at some point: the build fell to the VM because the
# inner map captures `i`, and the conversion boxed every element.
n = 3
a = tensor((0..n).map(i => (0..n).map(j => ((i * j) % 100) * 0.5)))
b = tensor((0..n).map(i => (0..n).map(j => ((i + j) % 100) * 0.25)))
print(a)
print(b)
print(a.matmul(b))
print(a.matmul(b).shape())

# an identity multiply, where any element reordering in EITHER operand shows immediately
id = tensor([[1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0], [0, 0, 1]])
print(a.matmul(id))
